@h1 A manuscript in chapters h1@

Keep a long document as one file per chapter, shared vocabulary in its own file,
and a thin master that assembles everything in order. Adding a chapter costs one
new file and two lines.

@h2 Step 1 — Shared vocabulary h2@

Put reusable templates and headings in @code shared/schema.wit code@, and
bibliographic anchors in @code shared/sources.wit code@. A block-form definition
declares its captures in double bars and wraps whatever the master splices in at
the @code ... code@ point.

@@@pre
~ shared/schema.wit

#cite: ::author:: (::year::) !!

#section ||title||
@h2 ::title:: h2@
...
section#
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 Put a blank line after any comment that is the first line
inside a definition body — without it, the following block collapses into a stray
paragraph. aside@

@h2 Step 2 — One file per chapter h2@

Each chapter references the shared files, then wraps its body in a block-form
definition so the master can place it wherever it likes.

@@@pre
~ chapters/01-introduction.wit

reference ../shared/schema.wit
reference ../shared/sources.wit

#chapter_one
@section |title The problem|

Attention, as any careful reader knows, is the rarest form of generosity.
chapter_one#
pre@@@

@h2 Step 3 — The master file h2@

Reference the shared files and every chapter, add single-line metadata, then emit
the chapters in order.

@@@pre
~ master.wit

reference ./shared/schema.wit
reference ./chapters/00-frontmatter.wit
reference ./chapters/01-introduction.wit

#thesis: { title - Attention as a Moral Practice, author - T Greig, status - draft } !!

@h1 @thesis.title h1@

By @thesis.author

(if @thesis.status is draft)
@watermark()
(end)

@frontmatter frontmatter@
@chapter_one chapter_one@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 4 — Build the master h2@

@@@pre
wit build master.wit --fragment
pre@@@

Or @code -o thesis.pdf code@, or @code -o thesis.md code@ — the CLI infers the
format from the extension.

@strong Result. strong@ One document assembled from several files: the metadata
header, a draft watermark (because @code status code@ is @code draft code@), the
frontmatter, and every chapter in order. The worked @code examples/thesis code@
builds green and is the reference spine.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong No commas inside a record. strong@ Commas separate fields, so
@code subtitle - Attention, Perception code@ throws a malformed-record error.
Keep metadata comma-free, or model it as a collection. li@
@li @strong Prefer single-line records for metadata. strong@ A record spanning
several lines is not reliably classified as data under the current parser —
write it on one line. li@
@li @strong Emit real headings. strong@ If a section template lays down
@code ## code@-style text it renders as literal characters in HTML. Define the
header with @@@code @h2 ::title:: h2@ code@@@ for a genuine heading. li@
ul@

@h2 Variations h2@

Gate a draft with a conditional on @code status code@; share citations across
every chapter by referencing @code shared/sources.wit code@ — see
@a |href /docs/recipes/bibliography/| A bibliography across files a@.

@h2 See also h2@

@a |href /docs/recipes/bibliography/| A bibliography across files a@ builds a
self-gathering reference list across these same chapters;
@a |href /docs/recipes/thesis/| A thesis to a print-ready PDF a@ covers the
single-file case.
